<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Candaules</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 680 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Candaules was the last king of the Heraclid dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, killed around 680 BCE by Gyges, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how Candaules, proud of his wife's beauty, made his bodyguard Gyges see her naked, and how the queen then forced Gyges to kill the king and marry her. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> preserves a different legend, the ring of Gyges.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/candaules.jpg" alt="Candaules" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Etty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Etty_(1787%E2%80%931849)_%E2%80%93_Candaules,_King_of_Lydia,_Shews_his_Wife_by_Stealth_to_Gyges,_One_of_his_Ministers,_as_She_Goes_to_Bed_%E2%80%93_N00358_%E2%80%93_Tate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Candaules, also known as Myrsilos, was a king of the ancient Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in the early years of the 7th century BCE. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, he succeeded his father Meles as the 22nd and last king of the Heraclid dynasty. His name was traditionally assumed, from an ambiguous line of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hipponax">Hipponax</a>, to mean hound-choker in Lydian, and J. B. Bury and Russell Meiggs describe it as a Maeonian name with that meaning. More recently it has been suggested that the name or title Kandaules is cognate with the Luwian hantawatt(i), meaning king, and probably has Carian origin. It gave its name to candaulism, a sexual practice which legend attributed to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Several accounts tell how the Heraclid dynasty ended and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a> of Gyges began, mostly in a mythical vein. The earliest is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s, of the fifth century BCE. He says Candaules thought his wife the most beautiful woman on Earth. He does not name her, though later artists and writers have called her Nyssia. Candaules urged his favourite bodyguard, Gyges, to see her naked, and Gyges, who first refused out of respect for the queen, hid in the bedroom and watched her undress. She saw him leave. Herodotus remarks that among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a>, as among most non-Greek peoples, it was thought highly indecent even for a man to be seen naked.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The queen swore revenge. Next day she gave Gyges two choices: kill Candaules and take the throne with her as his wife, or be executed at once by her trusted servants. Gyges hid in the bedroom again and stabbed the sleeping king. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> at first did not accept him, so to prevent civil war Gyges offered to let the Delphic Oracle confirm or refuse his position, agreeing to restore the throne to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracleidae">Heraclids</a> if it declared against him. The Oracle supported him, adding that the Heraclids would have their revenge in the fifth generation of the Mermnadae. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-republic">Plato's Republic</a> a different story has Gyges use a magical ring of invisibility to usurp the throne.</p><p>Bury and Meiggs reject <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s tale and hold that the family of Candaules, though descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> himself, had become degenerate, so that Gyges assassinated him and began a new era under his own Mermnad clan. Herodotus's version appears in Michael Ondaatje's 1992 novel The English Patient, and Alexander von Zemlinsky's opera Der König Kandaules is based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-gide">André Gide</a>'s play Le roi Candaule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Candaules” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom becomes historical with Gyges, who seized the throne around 680 BCE by killing the last Heraclid king, <b>Candaules</b>; Herodotus tells a famous tale of how Candaules’ wife made Gyges choose between death and regicide.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The word tyrannos was probably borrowed: it may have come from Lydian or another language of Asia Minor, and one of its earliest uses is by the poet Archilochus, writing about Gyges of Lydia, who had killed King <b>Candaules</b> and taken his throne and his queen.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
